Open a ticket's body in $EDITOR and rewrite desc: to match the new last
line on save. This is the command that keeps the derived line number
honest; without it, every hand-edit is a chance for the header to start
lying about where the body ends.

Takes the lock from 8, since it rewrites the same file claim does. Should
also be usable non-interactively so an agent can replace a body and get the
header fixed for free.

Retitled in effect by 20: desc: is gone, so there is no derived line for edit
to keep honest and nothing a hand-edit can falsify. What is left is
ergonomics, and that is what was built.

pidly edit N opens info in $EDITOR. The editor works on a copy, outside the
lock — a lock held for a human edit blocks every claim on the ticket for as
long as the session lasts — and the install takes the lock, refuses if info
changed underneath (a claim landing on line 1 is the likely one), and refuses
a saved file that is no longer a ticket. A refused edit is kept and its path
printed; the original is untouched either way.

Non-interactively: -b replaces the body from stdin, -a appends to it. Both
explicit, because a body is the one thing in a ticket nothing can reconstruct
and 'pidly edit 7 < /dev/null' should not be able to empty it by accident.

The structural rules are check's, so they moved into info_faults() and check
now calls it too — the same mistake in_closed() was the fix for in 22.

Done: bin/pidly, covered by tests/edit.sh (27 cases, including a mangled save
and an edit racing a claim).
